+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in O(1) memory
+
+## What is required
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median(feed())` to:
+
+1. consume a **single-pass generator** of 200,000 arbitrary floats,
+2. return the **exact** median (matching a full sort of the feed), and
+3. allocate **less than 64 KB** of Python memory (traced by `tracemalloc`).
+
+`median()` receives no re-iterable input, no known value range, and no
+approximate-error tolerance.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+An exact median of an unrestricted stream cannot be computed in constant
+memory in a single pass. This is a well-known lower bound for selection in the
+streaming model: any exact algorithm must retain `Ω(n)` state (Munro and
+Paterson, "Selection and sorting with limited storage", 1980). Intuitively, to
+know which value is the middle one you must be able to distinguish it from
+every other value; with arbitrary floats there is no finite summary that
+preserves that ordering information for an unbounded number of inputs.
+
+For the test's 200,000 distinct floats, merely storing the values as Python
+floats costs about 1.6 MB (plus list overhead), which is roughly 25x the
+64 KB budget. `tracemalloc` will therefore always observe more than 64 KB for
+any implementation that keeps enough information to be exact. Meeting the
+memory bound necessarily means dropping information, which makes the result
+approximate and unable to equal the exact `want` computed by the test.
+
+The three requirements are mutually exclusive: **exact**, **single-pass
+streaming**, and **constant memory** — pick at most two.
+
+## Why this is not worked around
+
+The usual ways to make the test pass would violate its intent rather than
+satisfy it:
+
+- **External/disk-backed sort**: memory stays low in `tracemalloc`, but it uses
+  unbounded external storage, so it is not constant-memory streaming and would
+  not help the ingest workers.
+- **Approximate sketches** (t-digest, P², reservoir sampling): O(1) memory, but
+  not exact and will not match `want`.
+- **Multiple passes / sorting**: requires a re-iterable input (the test passes
+  a generator) and still uses O(n) memory.
+- **Inspecting the generator to regenerate the feed**: depends on the test's
+  private construction and is not a median algorithm.
+
+Per the task instructions, `BLOCKED.md` is recorded instead of applying one of
+these workarounds.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+The even-count bug was real and is fixed: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns
+`2.5` (the mean of the two middle values) instead of the upper middle element
+`3`.
+
+## Options for infra
+
+- Relax the requirement to an **approximate** streaming median with a stated
+  error bound (constant memory is achievable).
+- Allow **O(n) memory** for exactness (current behavior).
+- Provide a **re-iterable, seekable** input and allow multiple passes plus a
+  bounded-memory exact selection algorithm — still O(n) in the worst case for
+  arbitrary streams, or require a known bounded value domain so a histogram
+  can be exact.
